<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daud al-Fatani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1769–1847</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Dawud al-Fatani, Daud bin Abdullah al-Fatani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daud al-Fatani was a Malay Islamic scholar from Pattani who spent most of his life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, where he taught and wrote. He produced dozens of works in Malay, written in Jawi script, on law, theology and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>, which were widely used in religious schools across the Malay world. He is regarded as one of the most important Malay religious scholars of the 19th century.</p></div>
